<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The selected control: the held sculpt tool, the current Tool Rail entry, the
  active body's row, the drafted primitive, the chosen appearance.

  FILL-LED, and since UI-R4B fill-led with nothing else. What says "this is the
  one you are on" is the surface itself — `fsAccentFill`, the palette's
  selected-at-rest value, which is clearly lifted and clearly cooler or bluer
  than the control beside it — plus the brightened label that comes with it from
  `control_content_tint`.

  The accent hairline is GONE. It was described as the third signal and was
  read as the first: at a glance a selected chip was a blue-outlined box, six of
  them made a panel look like a form of framed cells, and the outline did no
  work the fill and the brightened label were not already doing. What remains is
  a state, not a box.

  The accent itself is still spent on exactly two things — a primary commit, and
  the ring on a focused numeric field — which is the distinction the hairline was
  quietly eroding.

  `fsAccentBorder` is consequently referenced by no drawable. It is deliberately
  left declared in `attrs.xml` and answered by all three palettes: those twelve
  values are owner-approved, and dropping one because the current composition
  does not spend it would change an approved palette to record a styling
  decision. The role stays; what changed is what draws with it.
-->
<selector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:state_pressed="true">
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="?attr/fsAccentFillPressed" />
            <corners android:radius="@dimen/radius_control" />
        </shape>
    </item>
    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="?attr/fsAccentFill" />
            <corners android:radius="@dimen/radius_control" />
        </shape>
    </item>
</selector>
